faqs:
  - question: "Do I need to change my site to stream responses?"
    answer: "No Appwrite-specific configuration is needed if your framework already streams HTML. A server-rendered site streams whenever its framework writes the page in parts, as Next.js does with Suspense and `loading.js`, SvelteKit with streamed promises, and Astro by default. Responses that carry a `Content-Length`, such as static files, are sent in one piece as before."
  - question: "How does a function stream its response?"
    answer: "Return `res.sendStream()` with a source of chunks, such as an async generator in Node.js, a generator in Python or PHP, a `Flow` in Kotlin, or an `AsyncSequence` in Swift. In Node.js, Bun, and Deno you can also return a Fetch API `Response` with a `ReadableStream` body. The stream reaches clients that call the function through its domain."
  - question: "Which function runtimes support streaming?"
    answer: "Every runtime except Rust and the deprecated `node-14.5`, `dart-2.15`, and `swift-5.5` runtimes. Go functions need `github.com/open-runtimes/types-for-go/v4` version `v4.0.11` or later."
  - question: "Do executions created with the SDK stream?"
    answer: "No. An execution created with the Create execution endpoint, directly or through an SDK, returns the whole body once the stream has ended. Call the function through its domain to receive the stream."
  - question: "How long can a stream run?"
    answer: "A stream is part of one execution, so it ends when the timeout is reached. On Appwrite Cloud, a site's stream can run until the site's timeout, up to 30 seconds, and a function's stream through its domain is limited to the shorter of the function's timeout and 60 seconds. For updates that run longer, use Appwrite Realtime."
  - question: "Can I turn streaming off on self-hosted Appwrite?"
    answer: "Yes. Streaming is on by default from the first self-hosted release that includes it. Set `_APP_COMPUTE_STREAMING` to `disabled` in your `.env` file to send each response only once it is complete."
---

Appwrite Sites and Functions now stream their responses. Each chunk your code writes reaches the browser as soon as it is ready, so your users can see a page's layout while slower sections load, or start reading an AI answer while the model writes the rest.

Streaming is enabled on Appwrite Cloud for every project. Server-rendered sites stream when their framework renders HTML in parts, and functions stream when they return a stream, in every runtime except Rust.

# What streaming makes possible

- **Pages that render before all their data is loaded.** Frameworks such as Next.js, SvelteKit, Astro, Remix, and TanStack Start can send the page shell first and fill in slower sections as their data arrives. Your users see the layout and navigation right away instead of a blank tab.
- **AI responses as the model writes them.** Chat interfaces, summaries, and agents can show text as the model generates it, so your users start reading before the full answer is ready.
- **Progress updates for short jobs.** With server-sent events, a function can report each step of an import, a report, or a batch of API calls while the work is still running.
- **Large responses without building them first.** A function can write an export or a generated file chunk by chunk, so the download starts with the first rows instead of after the last one.

# Streaming in Appwrite Sites

If your framework streams HTML in development, it streams the same way on Appwrite Sites, with no adapter settings or configuration flags.

This Next.js page sends its heading immediately and the order list once the query behind it finishes:

```jsx
import { Suspense } from 'react';

async function Orders() {
    const orders = await getOrders();
    return <OrderList orders={orders} />;
}

export default function Page() {
    return (
        <main>
            <h1>Dashboard</h1>
            <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading orders...</p>}>
                <Orders />
            </Suspense>
        </main>
    );
}
```

Streamed pages are still compressed with gzip or Brotli when the browser supports it. Static files and other responses with a known size are sent in one piece, as before.

# Streaming in Appwrite Functions

A function streams when it returns `res.sendStream()` with a source of chunks, followed by an optional status code and headers. The status code and headers are sent with the first chunk.

Libraries that already return a stream plug straight in. With the [Vercel AI SDK](https://ai-sdk.dev), pass the model's text stream to `sendStream()` and the reply reaches the browser as the model writes it:

```server-nodejs
import { streamText } from 'ai';
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';

export default async ({ req, res }) => {
    const result = streamText({
        model: openai('gpt-5-mini'),
        prompt: req.bodyJson.prompt
    });

    return res.sendStream(result.textStream, 200, {
        'content-type': 'text/plain; charset=utf-8'
    });
};
```

Call the function through its domain to receive the chunks as they are written. With curl, pass `-N` so each chunk prints as it arrives:

```bash
curl -N -X POST https://<FUNCTION_DOMAIN> \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -d '{"prompt": "Explain streaming in three sentences."}'
```

The Create execution endpoint, including calls through an SDK, returns the complete body after the stream ends.

`sendStream()` takes the source of chunks your runtime already uses, such as an async generator in Node.js, a generator in Python and PHP, a `Flow` in Kotlin, or an `AsyncSequence` in Swift. This function sends three server-sent events, one second apart:

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
export default async ({ res }) => {
    async function* events() {
        for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
            if (i > 1) {
                await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 1000));
            }
            yield `data: Message ${i}\n\n`;
        }
    }

    return res.sendStream(events(), 200, {
        'content-type': 'text/event-stream'
    });
};
```
```php
<?php

return function ($context) {
    $events = (function () {
        for ($i = 1; $i <= 3; $i++) {
            if ($i > 1) {
                sleep(1);
            }
            yield "data: Message {$i}\n\n";
        }
    })();

    return $context->res->sendStream($events, 200, [
        'content-type' => 'text/event-stream'
    ]);
};
```
```python
import time

def main(context):
    def events():
        for i in range(1, 4):
            if i > 1:
                time.sleep(1)
            yield f"data: Message {i}\n\n"

    return context.res.sendStream(events(), 200, {
        "content-type": "text/event-stream"
    })
```
```ruby
def main(context)
    events = Enumerator.new do |out|
        (1..3).each do |i|
            sleep 1 if i > 1
            out << "data: Message #{i}\n\n"
        end
    end

    return context.res.send_stream(events, 200, {
        'content-type' => 'text/event-stream'
    })
end
```
```deno
export default async ({ res }: any) => {
    async function* events() {
        for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
            if (i > 1) {
                await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 1000));
            }
            yield `data: Message ${i}\n\n`;
        }
    }

    return res.sendStream(events(), 200, {
        "content-type": "text/event-stream"
    });
};
```
```go
package handler

import (
	"fmt"
	"time"

	"github.com/open-runtimes/types-for-go/v4/openruntimes"
)

func Main(Context openruntimes.Context) openruntimes.Response {
	events := func(yield func([]byte, error) bool) {
		for i := 1; i <= 3; i++ {
			if i > 1 {
				time.Sleep(time.Second)
			}
			if !yield([]byte(fmt.Sprintf("data: Message %d\n\n", i)), nil) {
				return
			}
		}
	}

	return Context.Res.SendStream(
		events,
		Context.Res.WithStatusCode(200),
		Context.Res.WithHeaders(map[string]string{"content-type": "text/event-stream"}),
	)
}
```
```dart
import 'dart:async';

Future<dynamic> main(final context) async {
    Stream<String> events() async* {
        for (var i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
            if (i > 1) {
                await Future.delayed(Duration(seconds: 1));
            }
            yield 'data: Message $i\n\n';
        }
    }

    return context.res.sendStream(events(), 200, {
        'content-type': 'text/event-stream'
    });
}
```
```swift
import Foundation

func main(context: RuntimeContext) async throws -> RuntimeOutput {
    let events = AsyncStream<String> { continuation in
        Task {
            for i in 1...3 {
                if i > 1 {
                    try? await Task.sleep(nanoseconds: 1_000_000_000)
                }
                continuation.yield("data: Message \(i)\n\n")
            }
            continuation.finish()
        }
    }

    return context.res.sendStream(events, statusCode: 200, headers: [
        "content-type": "text/event-stream"
    ])
}
```
```csharp
namespace DotNetRuntime;

public class Handler {
    public async Task<RuntimeOutput> Main(RuntimeContext Context)
    {
        async IAsyncEnumerable<object> Events()
        {
            for (var i = 1; i <= 3; i++)
            {
                if (i > 1)
                {
                    await Task.Delay(1000);
                }
                yield return $"data: Message {i}\n\n";
            }
        }

        return Context.Res.SendStream(Events(), 200, new Dictionary<string, string>() {
            { "content-type", "text/event-stream" }
        });
    }
}
```
```kotlin
package io.openruntimes.kotlin.src

import io.openruntimes.kotlin.RuntimeContext
import io.openruntimes.kotlin.RuntimeOutput
import kotlinx.coroutines.delay
import kotlinx.coroutines.flow.flow

class Main {
    fun main(context: RuntimeContext): RuntimeOutput {
        val events = flow {
            for (i in 1..3) {
                if (i > 1) {
                    delay(1000)
                }
                emit("data: Message $i\n\n")
            }
        }

        return context.res.sendStream(events, 200, mapOf("content-type" to "text/event-stream"))
    }
}
```
```java
package io.openruntimes.java.src;

import io.openruntimes.java.RuntimeContext;
import io.openruntimes.java.RuntimeOutput;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.Map;

public class Main {
    public RuntimeOutput main(RuntimeContext context) throws Exception {
        Iterable<String> events = () -> new Iterator<String>() {
            private int i = 0;

            @Override
            public boolean hasNext() {
                return i < 3;
            }

            @Override
            public String next() {
                if (i > 0) {
                    try {
                        Thread.sleep(1000);
                    } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                        throw new RuntimeException(e);
                    }
                }
                i++;
                return "data: Message " + i + "\n\n";
            }
        };

        Map<String, String> headers = new HashMap<>();
        headers.put("content-type", "text/event-stream");

        return context.getRes().sendStream(events, 200, headers);
    }
}
```
```cpp
#include "../RuntimeResponse.h"
#include "../RuntimeRequest.h"
#include "../RuntimeOutput.h"
#include "../RuntimeContext.h"

#include <chrono>
#include <optional>
#include <string>
#include <thread>

namespace runtime {
  class Handler {
    public:
      static RuntimeOutput main(RuntimeContext &context) {
        Json::Value headers;
        headers["content-type"] = "text/event-stream";

        return context.res.sendStream([i = 0]() mutable -> std::optional<std::string> {
          i++;
          if (i > 3) {
            return std::nullopt;
          }
          if (i > 1) {
            std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));
          }
          return "data: Message " + std::to_string(i) + "\n\n";
        }, 200, headers);
      }
  };
}
```
{% /multicode %}

In Node.js, Bun, and Deno, a function can also return a Fetch API `Response` with a `ReadableStream` body, and in Dart, a [shelf](https://pub.dev/packages/shelf) `Response`. Responses sent with `res.send()`, `res.json()`, and the other response methods are sent in one piece, as before.

# Timeouts and limits

- **Streams end at the timeout.** On Appwrite Cloud, a site's stream can run until the site's timeout, up to 30 seconds. A function's stream through its domain is limited to the shorter of the function's timeout and 60 seconds. The execution keeps running while the stream is open, so a stream counts toward execution time like any other response. For updates that run longer, use [Appwrite Realtime](/docs/apis/realtime).
- **Preview URLs send pages in one piece.** HTML pages on a preview URL that shows the Appwrite banner are not streamed. Use the site's own domain to see a page stream.
- **Close an `EventSource` on your last event.** A browser `EventSource` reconnects when the response ends, which runs your function again. Send a final event, and call `close()` on the `EventSource` when your page receives it.
- **Rust is not supported yet.** Neither are the deprecated `node-14.5`, `dart-2.15`, and `swift-5.5` runtimes. Go functions need `types-for-go` `v4.0.11` or later.

# Streaming on self-hosted Appwrite

Streaming ships in the next self-hosted release and is on by default for sites and functions. To send each response only once it is complete, set the `_APP_COMPUTE_STREAMING` [environment variable](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/configuration/environment-variables#compute) to `disabled`.

